---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../../components/TableScroll.astro'
import { Badge } from 'web-design-system/components'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Recipes and runs', href: '#recipes' },
  { label: 'Nodes and edges', href: '#nodes' },
  { label: 'How a step becomes a task', href: '#materialization' },
  { label: 'The run lifecycle', href: '#lifecycle' },
  { label: 'The coding pipeline', href: '#coding' },
  { label: 'Branches and worktrees', href: '#git' },
  { label: 'The loopback tool server', href: '#mcp' },
  { label: 'Prompts', href: '#prompts' },
  { label: 'The automations panel', href: '#panel' },
]

// Tuples, not objects: Panda treats object literals under `src/` as style
// candidates, so records with keys like `content` become bogus CSS.
const recipeFields: [string, string][] = [
  ['id, slug', 'Identity, and the name the recipe is referred to by.'],
  ['version', 'Monotonically increasing. A recipe is immutable and versioned, so a run pins an exact one.'],
  ['recipe_json', 'The graph itself: the nodes and the edges between them.'],
]

const runFields: [string, string][] = [
  ['recipe_id, params', 'Which recipe, and this run\u2019s values for its parameters.'],
  ['status', 'active, completed, or cancelled.'],
  ['step_results', 'Node id to result, written as each step finishes. The reserved @notes key holds a coding run\u2019s ordered log of annotations, rejections, approvals, spec saves, and merges.'],
  ['root_task_id', 'For a coding run, the feature task it is attached to. Its phase steps materialize as that task\u2019s subtasks.'],
  ['branch, base_branch, branch_status', 'The feature branch, where it was cut from, and where it is in its life.'],
]

const nodeFields: [string, string][] = [
  ['id, kind', 'The step, and whether it is an action or an event wait.'],
  ['ai, approval', 'Whether an agent does the work, and whether a person has to sign it off.'],
  ['phase', 'For coding recipes: interview, spec, implement, review, or merge.'],
  ['subtask', 'Whether the step becomes a subtask of the run\u2019s root task rather than a standalone task.'],
  ['retrigger_on_reject, retrigger_node', 'Whether a rejection can reopen an earlier step, and which one if it is not simply the feeder.'],
]

const edgeFields: [string, string][] = [
  ['from, to', 'The step being left and the step being entered.'],
  ['condition_type', 'on_complete, on_result, timer, or event.'],
  ['condition_value', 'The value the condition is matched against, for on_result edges.'],
]

const gitOps: [string, string][] = [
  ['is_repo, changed_paths', 'Whether a directory is a repository, and which tracked or untracked files differ.'],
  ['current_branch, branch_exists, switch_branch', 'Branch inspection and checkout.'],
  ['worktree_add', 'Adds a worktree on a new branch cut from a base. This is where a run\u2019s feature branch is actually created.'],
  ['worktree_remove, worktree_prune', 'Removes a worktree, refusing when it holds uncommitted work; prunes entries whose checkout was deleted by hand.'],
  ['merge_no_ff, abort_merge, delete_branch', 'Merge with a commit so the feature boundary stays visible, abort one, and delete a branch.'],
  ['push_branch, stage_and_commit', 'Push a branch and set its upstream; stage everything and commit.'],
  ['resolve_remote, parse_github_remote, git_common_dir, ensure_excluded', 'Find the remote and its owner/repo, locate the common git directory, and add the worktree path to git\u2019s exclude file.'],
]

const serverTools: [string, string][] = [
  ['attach the spec', 'Save the interviewed spec onto the feature task.'],
  ['create and interview sub-tasks', 'Split work out of the feature and start a run on it.'],
  ['log annotations', 'Append to the run\u2019s ordered note log.'],
  ['propose a branch name', 'Record the branch the run should use. The app creates it once the spec is approved.'],
  ['report phase completion', 'Move the run on to the next phase.'],
]

const phases: string[] = ['interview', 'spec', 'implement', 'review', 'merge']
---

<Docs
  title="Workflow engine"
  description="Recipes, runs, and how a workflow turns into ordinary tasks — plus the coding pipeline that runs on top of it, its git handling, and the panel that drives it."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    The workflow engine is how a repeatable process — a coding feature, a trip checklist, a
    recurring chore — becomes concrete tasks with state of their own. It lives in{' '}
    <code>libs/storage/src/workflow.rs</code>. The reader-facing description of what workflows
    do is in <a href="/docs/workflows">the user guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recipes">Recipes and runs</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>recipe</strong> is the blueprint, stored as a graph. A <strong>run</strong> is one
    instance of it:
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="Recipe fields">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {recipeFields.map(([field, detail]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{field}</code></td>
            <td>{detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Run field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {runFields.map(([field, detail]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{field}</code></td>
          <td>{detail}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Versioning is the point of the recipe split: a run records the version it started on, so
    editing a recipe can never change the meaning of a run already in flight.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nodes">Nodes and edges</h2>
  <p>
    A recipe is a directed acyclic graph. Each <code>RecipeNode</code> is a step, and each{' '}
    <code>RecipeEdge</code> says when the next one is entered.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Node field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {nodeFields.map(([field, detail]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{field}</code></td>
          <td>{detail}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Edge field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {edgeFields.map(([field, detail]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{field}</code></td>
          <td>{detail}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="materialization">How a step becomes a task</h2>
  <p>
    There is no separate workflow-task entity. A step <em>is</em> an ordinary task row carrying{' '}
    <code>workflow_run_id</code> and <code>node_id</code>, which is why a workflow step shows up
    in your list, can be ticked, and needs no special rendering.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Lazy</strong> — a step's row is created only once its prerequisites are satisfied,
      so a run does not dump its whole future onto the list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Time waits</strong> — a downstream action task is created with{' '}
      <code>blocked_until = completion + duration</code>. The list hides anything more than two
      days out, so the waiting is invisible until it is nearly actionable rather than a wall of
      tasks you cannot start.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Event waits</strong> — a visible, tickable task, resolved by a webhook, a button,
      or you ticking it. One representation for "something outside has to happen", whatever the
      source.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coding runs</strong> — the phase steps materialize as subtasks of the run's root
      task, so the feature and its phases stay one unit in the list.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="lifecycle">The run lifecycle</h2>
  <p>
    Three operations drive a run. <code>complete_workflow_step(task_id, result)</code> marks a
    step's task done, records its result against the node, and then evaluates the outgoing edges
    to decide what to materialize next — the engine advances by completing steps, not by a
    scheduler ticking in the background. <code>fire_workflow_event(run_id, name)</code> resolves
    an event wait. <code>cancel_workflow_run(run_id)</code> tombstones every task belonging to
    the run and marks it cancelled.
  </p>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Cancelling keeps the branch">
    A cancelled coding run stays visible with its branch name rather than disappearing, so the
    branch is cleaned up deliberately instead of being orphaned in your repository.
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Rejection is the fourth path, and the one that makes review loops work. When an approval step
    rejects and the node allows it, the engine reopens an earlier step — the feeder, or a
    specific <code>retrigger_node</code> — and tombstones that step's still-pending children, so
    the reopened work starts from a clean slate rather than inheriting answers that were just
    rejected.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coding">The coding pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    The coding workflow is a recipe whose nodes carry a phase. There are five:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      phases.map((phase) => (
        <li>
          <code>{phase}</code>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    The task details pane renders them as a stepper. Each phase's state is derived, not stored:
    Active means it has an open step, Done means every step in it is complete, otherwise
    Pending. The round number in labels like <Badge tone="accent" variant="subtle">
      Round 2 · Implement
    </Badge> is counted from the rejections recorded in the run's notes, so going back a round
    is visible as history rather than a silent reset.
  </p>
  <p>
    A rejection reopens the interview step, which is what lets a feature be re-specified instead
    of patched after the fact.
  </p>

  <h2 id="git">Branches and worktrees</h2>
  <p>
    <code>apps/todo-2/src/coding_git.rs</code> shells out to <code>git</code>. Every function is
    synchronous and expects to be called from a blocking context, and failures carry git's own
    stderr so the step that failed can show you why.
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="Git operations">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Operation</th><th>Does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {gitOps.map(([operation, detail]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{operation}</code></td>
            <td>{detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The feature branch is created by adding a <em>worktree</em> for it, not by switching the
    checkout you are working in — so a coding run cannot move the branch out from under your own
    editor. Before that, the worktree's path is written into git's exclude file, so the main
    checkout never shows the new files as untracked noise.
  </p>
  <p>
    Removing a worktree refuses when it holds uncommitted changes: you are never silently
    deleted out of an edit. The branch itself survives removal, which is what keeps it in the
    panel's cleanup list.
  </p>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Your credentials, not the API token">
    Pushing a branch uses your own git credentials. The provider's API token never reaches git,
    a remote URL, or a credential helper.
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <code>branch_status</code> moves a run's branch through its life: proposed by the agent,
    active once created, then merged, deleted, or abandoned. The statuses exist so a branch can
    be accounted for after its run is gone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp">The loopback tool server</h2>
  <p>
    The agent cannot touch the store directly. Instead the app hosts a minimal MCP server on
    loopback, bound to an ephemeral port on <code>127.0.0.1</code> with a per-process bearer
    token, started on a dedicated thread. It implements only the three methods the coding flow
    needs: <code>initialize</code>, <code>tools/list</code>, and <code>tools/call</code>.
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="What the tool server lets the agent do">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {serverTools.map(([tool, detail]) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{tool}</td>
            <td>{detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    Those calls mutate the store and then notify the foreground application to refresh, so the
    UI keeps up with what the agent is doing mid-phase instead of waiting for the turn to end.
    On the agent's side, the same tools are reached as MCP tools; the servers you configure
    yourself are a separate set — see <a href="/docs/contributor/integrations">Integrations</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prompts">Prompts</h2>
  <p>
    Phases are driven by prompts the app composes and drops into the agent pane's prompt box for
    you to edit before sending; nothing is sent on your behalf. The interview prompt is the
    substantial one, and it asks the agent to gather context by reading files and searching the
    codebase, ask clarifying questions over several rounds, write the spec to{' '}
    <code>./docs/spec/&lt;slug&gt;-spec.md</code>, call <code>save_spec</code> when it is
    available, and reply with a summary and the path it chose.
  </p>
  <p>
    A later round of an interview gets told which round it is and why the previous one was
    rejected, so a re-spec starts from the objection rather than from scratch. The implement
    prompt is much shorter — implement the approved spec — because by then the spec carries the
    detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a feature has open subtasks, the interview prompt requires a decision on every one of
    them: either give it its own spec, or mark it explicitly covered by the feature's. Leaving
    one undecided is not allowed, which is what stops a feature from quietly shipping with
    unexamined sub-work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="panel">The automations panel</h2>
  <p>
    <code>AutomationsPanel</code> is the UI over all of this, and it replaced the older
    workflows page rather than sitting beside it. It lists the available recipes as automations,
    lists active runs grouped by the recipe they came from, and — for an active run — presents
    the steps that need a decision, with the buttons the step's outgoing edges imply: approve or
    reject where the edge is <code>on_result</code>, complete where it is{' '}
    <code>on_complete</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a coding run it shows the current phase and gives you the branch housekeeping, including
    deleting a branch. It refreshes from the store rather than holding its own copy, and emits a
    changed event when a run moves or ownership changes, so the task list and the tag tree
    update too.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/contributor/data-model">The data model</a> covers the tables these runs are
    stored in, and <a href="/docs/contributor/agent-runtime">the agent runtime</a> covers the
    other side of the loopback server.
  </p>
</Docs>
